4.84, 76.0], [901.15, 1223.1, 74.83], [906.98, 1221.79, 73.4], [912.56, 1220.59, 71.52], [917.2, 1219.64, 69.48], [921.76, 1218.75, 66.98], [925.62, 1218.03, 64.44], [929.47, 1217.35, 61.48], [932.74, 1216.81, 58.6], [936.02, 1216.3, 55.37], [938.82, 1215.89, 52.31], [941.66, 1215.51, 48.94], [944.1, 1215.2, 45.8], [946.58, 1214.91, 42.39], [948.74, 1214.68, 39.22], [950.95, 1214.46, 35.8], [952.88, 1214.29, 32.64], [954.86, 1214.12, 29.23], [956.61, 1213.99, 26.09], [958.41, 1213.87, 22.7], [960.0, 1213.77, 19.58], [961.64, 1213.67, 16.21], [963.09, 1213.59, 13.1], [964.58, 1213.52, 9.72], [965.9, 1213.46, 6.53], [967.25, 1213.4, 2.99], [968.29, 1213.36, 0.0]]}]}, "agent_error": null, "agent_extra": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "served_by": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "fallbacks": false, "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "effort": "high", "notes": [{"before_call": 2, "text": "I'm planning test drops at several altitudes, since measured wind aloft should reveal shear and veer directly, and fall time and impact should give drag. I'm also considering drops through the smoke column at the origin to check for updrafts, starting with drops away from the fire.\n\nI'm going to start with a test drop at altitude 100 and speed 45, released well away from the fire at x=400, and I'll watch whether it drifts into the smoke."}, {"before_call": 5, "text": "I'm working out the distance, which comes to 565. Using the point (300, -300) gives a result of 424, and that checks out."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm checking the fall times first: 100 m in 7.76 s is much slower than free fall's 4.5 s, so drag must be significant, and I'm testing whether that means terminal velocity, which comes out to about 14 m/s with a drag coefficient near 0.05. Next I'm looking at the wind readings at 40, 100, and 150 m, where the ratio 7.9/6.7 \u2248 1.18 gives me the shear exponent.\n\nI'm working out the wind shear exponent from the three wind readings, and the numbers give inconsistent values, so I'm settling on roughly 0.35 with negligible veer and a wind direction near 91\u201392\u00b0. Next I want to test drag at other speeds and headings, so I'm planning drops through the smoke from different altitudes and a faster, reversed-heading run, starting from a release point offset upstream of the fire."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "If fall times are running long, the drag is probably set too high, since more drag means a slower fall. I'll lower it and try 0.035."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I'm settling on a drag value around 0.037, since the zero crossings from the earlier tests land between roughly 0.034 and 0.0385. The negative dx suggests wind may be stronger than I assumed, so I'll run a few more informative drops before fitting again.\n\nI'm planning the next drops: a couple of smoke tests at altitudes 100 and 150, and a fast one at speed 80 heading 180 to check how initial velocity affects the drag estimate."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "The fall time in test 4 came out at 9.0 seconds against an expected 7.76, so the updraft seems to be slowing the drop by about 1.24 seconds, which would put its speed somewhere around 3 to 4 m/s. The landing at y = -2 instead of 0 is also a bit odd, though I'll set that aside for now. Next I want to compare against the run with no updraft, which gave 0.037, and then add the updraft back in."}, {"before_call": 13, "text": "The reading is a bit under target, with the updraft needing a small bump, so I'll raise it to 4 and set the wind speed to 7 for the next trial."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "I've got a rough estimate of the updraft now. What's still unclear is how wind veer and shear change with altitude, and how drag behaves at low speed and low altitude. I'm planning a few more test runs at different headings, altitudes, and speeds to pin those down.\n\nThe wind direction readings at three altitudes all come out nearly the same, so veer looks like it's essentially zero and I can set that aside. For the remaining drops I'll try low altitude at heading 270, high altitude at heading 90, and a mid-altitude pass at heading 135 that crosses the edge of the smoke."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "I'm nudging the parameters: wind is probably a bit stronger than it needs to be or drag a bit too low, since most of the errors come out negative. I'll try wind at 7.2 with drag at 0.036, and I'm also wondering whether drag should go up a little, because several predicted times are shorter than expected. Test 6 is the odd one at low altitude, so I'll settle on drag 0.038 and wind 7.3."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "I'm settling on a drag of 0.037 and wind of 7.15, which is slightly worse on the dt side but a reasonable middle ground. I'll keep the previous hypothesis as the working set and leave the two test drops unused. I'm still wondering whether one more updraft check with a different smoke path would be worthwhile, since test 4 only has a single smoke sample.\n\nI'm going to use the last two drops on the smoke columns, since the updraft effect matters there. One would be a high release at 150 meters at slow speed for a long exposure, and the other a low release at 40 meters, off-center. I'll need to run simulate to find the release points, starting with altitude 150, heading 0, speed 45 from (-45, -65)."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "Getting started now, since the match looks good."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "I'm checking whether the simulate tool accepts an intensity argument, and I think the updraft parameter only sets the peak for intensity 1. The simulator seems to model just the calibration fire at the origin, not the real air. So for the targets I'd need to work out the landing offset by hand, and I'm considering a drop near the origin as a starting point.\n\nI'm weighing whether a drop near the origin can stand in for the targets, since the simulator only models the calibration smoke at (0,0). Aiming at the fire's centre means the load falls through smoke near the end of its path, and the smoke radius scales with intensity, which the tool can't reproduce directly, so I'd be approximating and accepting some error of a few metres.\n\nI'm now considering whether the trajectory can end at the fire's centre with the load drifting through the smoke only near landing. The load's horizontal speed drops to roughly wind speed quickly, so the final fall is mostly vertical and the smoke's size and shape matter. Simulating with scaled updraft only approximates the intensity-dependent radius, so I'd be accepting an error of a few metres.\n\nI'm thinking about whether to run the simulation with a scaled updraft to approximate the intensity-dependent smoke radius, and checking how sensitive the result is to that approximation. I also want to pick the heading, altitude and speed within their ranges, weighing that a lower drop means less time in the smoke, since the wind drift over the fall is what matters.\n\nI'm considering whether I can emulate a smaller fire radius by rescaling the problem, but the wind field doesn't scale cleanly, so that won't work. Instead I'll run the simulation with the effective updraft for each intensity and test how sensitive the landing point is when I vary that strength by about 20%.\n\nI'm working out how to place the release so the load lands on a target, and I'll treat the simulation's smoke at the origin as a stand-in. The idea is to simulate with the release offset from the target's fire and read off the landing point relative to it. I'm settling on a heading near the middle of its range, since heading matters little for the landing offset.\n\nI'm settling on a starting guess for the release point relative to the origin, then I'll simulate it, shift the release by the observed impact error, and repeat until it lands on the fire. For the first target I'm trying heading 84, altitude 55 and speed 67, with the release offset roughly 65 to 70 metres west of the fire."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "I'm shifting the release point left by 31.4 and down by 6.5, which puts the new starting position P0 at (-91.4, -6.5). Next I'll run the simulation from there."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "The smoke is what made this nonlinear, so I'm shifting the starting point to (-88.9, -6.5). I also want a sensitivity check: running the same point with updraft at 0 to isolate the smoke effect, and again at 3.5."}, {"before_call": 28, "text": "The low sensitivity of 0.7 m per 0.86 updraft is fine for this one, since the load only enters the smoke at the end at high speed. Target 1 release works out to about (1030.1, -299.5), though I want to double-check the y value, since impact is at y=0 and the release sits at -6.5 while the heading pushes north a little. I'll compute every release point first and only then do the drops, which are independent of each other.\n\nNow I'm going ahead with dropping the first load."}, {"before_call": 29, "text": "I'm working through target 2's numbers: altitude 120, speed 55, heading 239 southwest, with a guessed forward travel of about 60 and an eastward wind pushing back. From that I'm estimating a release point near (51, 31), though the starting position is still uncertain.\n\nI'm testing a different starting point of (0, 30) to see whether the drift-corrected position fits better."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "I'm shifting point P0 by -13.7 on x and +0.1 on y, which puts it at (-13.7, 30.1)."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "I'm weighing how sensitive the result is here, since a small change in the updraft shifts things quite a bit. A tighter turn radius should cut down on smoke exposure, so I'm leaning toward a lower altitude of 110 and a higher speed of 60 to shorten the time spent in the smoke, and I'll check the heading as well.\n\nI'm considering whether the effective updraft should be scaled down, since the real radius is smaller than the one I assumed and the outer updraft is weaker. I'll try an effective updraft of about 3.1 with altitude 110 and speed 60, then re-run the simulation to see how it changes the result."}, {"before_call": 33, "text": "Setting the point P0 at (-1.0, 31.3) as the shift."}, {"before_call": 35, "text": "I'm seeing the nonlinearity here, so I'm shifting the starting point back by about 3 to P0=(-3.5, 31.3), which should land near the middle of the uncertainty range.\n\nI'm settling on the release point at (-1153.5, 780.3), which is the earlier coordinate with the -3.5 shift applied to x and +31.3 to y."}, {"before_call": 36, "text": "I'm settling on altitude 95 and speed 65 for target 3, keeping heading 280, which gives a direction of roughly (-0.985, 0.174). With the wind blowing east, I expect the load to drift west first and then east, and I'm guessing the starting point P0 is around (60, -10)."}, {"before_call": 37, "text": "Impact at x=44 against a release of 60 only moved the load by -16 net, so I'm working out where P0 lands, which comes to (15.8, -9.7)."}, {"before_call": 38, "text": "I'm setting the shift to -2.6, which puts P0 at (13.2, -9.7). I'll re-run it with u=3.0 and u=2.0 to see how sensitive the result is."}, {"before_call": 40, "text": "I'm working out the release point as the sum of the two coordinate pairs, which comes to about (224.2, -1510.7). I'm also considering a small +0.5 nudge, and I'm leaning toward keeping it as is."}, {"before_call": 41, "text": "I'm working on target 4 now: its intensity is 0.72, and the effective value is 0.9 times 2.88, about 2.6. The heading is 27 degrees, altitude 110, speed 65, with direction (0.454, 0.891). I'm going to guess the starting point is (-20, -50)."}, {"before_call": 42, "text": "I'm working out the shifted coordinates: moving the point by (-42.1, -7.5) puts P0 at (-82.1, -57.5)."}, {"before_call": 43, "text": "I'm checking the starting point P0 at (-79.7, -58.0) and looking at how sensitive the result is to it."}, {"before_call": 45, "text": "Release: (-1435-79.7, -1164-58) = (-1514.7, -1222)."}, {"before_call": 46, "text": "I'm working on target 5: its intensity is 0.88, and the effective speed is 0.9 \u00d7 3.52 \u2248 3.2. Its heading is 105\u00b0, altitude 77, and speed 55, with direction (0.966, \u22120.259). I'm guessing its starting point is (\u221260, 15)."}, {"before_call": 47, "text": "I'm setting the starting point P0 at coordinates (-94.8, 16.8)."}, {"before_call": 48, "text": "I'm checking the starting point P0 = (-93.0, 16.9) and want to confirm it with a quick check, then run the same verification again using u = 2.5."}, {"before_call": 49, "text": "Release: (967-93, 1213+16.9) = (874, 1229.9)."}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 558172, "completion_tokens": 15720, "cache_read_tokens": 537351, "requests": 36}, "stopped": "done"}, "agent": "claude-sonnet-5-5-canadair-2026-11-14", "run": 3}